Summary of patch series in this merge:

- "maple_tree fixes for 7.3" (Liam R. Howlett):

  Prevent RCU readers from dereferencing NULL when a tree is emptied
  during iteration, including while reading /proc/PID/maps. Make
  internal warnings visible to syzbot and add a regression test.

- "mm/kasan: Detect double-free involving kfree_rcu_sheaf()."
  (Kuniyuki Iwashima):

  Detect a second free before a deferred RCU sheaf can poison an object
  which has since been reallocated. Report the offending free instead
  of a misleading use-after-free in the object's new owner.

- "mm: Fix page-table teardown during memory hot-remove"
  (Muchun Song):

  Match page-table destructors to their constructors when memory is
  removed. Fix NR_PAGETABLE accounting on x86, RISC-V and arm64 for
  tables allocated through different paths.

- "mm/damon: fix watermark state, kunit pid leak and selftest fake pass"
  (SJ Park):

  Resume DAMON monitoring when an online update removes the schemes
  whose watermarks kept it waiting. Fix leaked pid references in a
  KUnit test and make a skipped selftest report a skip rather than pass.

Summary of the 16 singletons in this merge:

The XArray and filemap fault-around fixes prevent a backwards page-cache
walk from reaching outside the locked page-table page, where it could
read freed memory or write out of bounds. Another XArray fix prevents
RCU walkers from following a new tree head into a freed parent.
Associative-array updates no longer hit a kernel BUG after collapsing
a leaf-only subtree. Moving a hugetlbfs trace event before superblock
teardown avoids a use-after-free when the event is enabled.

Two fixes address indefinite hangs: a stale large swap-cache folio can
make shmem swapin retry forever, and swapoff can leave reclaim looping
while waiting for writeback which reclaim has yet to submit. Three
userfaultfd fixes prevent missed wakeups after a partially completed
write-unprotect, failed unmaps which leave ioctls returning -EAGAIN, and
stale write-protection state in a forked child. Weighted interleave no
longer divides by zero when memory hotplug changes the available nodes.

Migration now moves the per-node FileHugePages and kernel-file page
counts with their folios, avoiding persistent accounting errors after
the folios are freed. The memory-failure path fixes a leaked folio
reference and protects a mapping lookup against concurrent truncation.
It also updates poison statistics before emitting the trace event, so
rasdaemon sees the new count when handling that event.
